- Without reinforcing a gender binary, the book points to the vast realms of technical history made invisible by the masculinist view of technologies as artifacts that work on matter rather than practices that shape bodies. Technology, as I wrote about one of Google’s more outlandish moves, is womb envy. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j148tn3xzspxdh679kvyt67t))
    - Note: In this context, "womb envy" refers to a concept where men might feel a subconscious envy of women's ability to give birth and nurture life. This term, a counterpart to Freud's concept of "penis envy," suggests that some technological endeavors, especially those led by male-dominated industries, may be driven by a desire to replicate or control aspects traditionally associated with women's reproductive capabilities. The author implies that certain technological innovations or initiatives, like those from Google, could be interpreted as attempts to compensate for or mimic this unique biological function. This idea challenges the traditional view of technology as merely a means of manipulating matter, suggesting that deeper psychological and societal motivations, related to gender dynamics, are at play in the development and application of technology.
- There are limits on how much you can impose on others for expert advice, though I obviously did so a lot. A super smart doctor friend of mine who chose a career first in family medicine and then in the ER says that general practitioners have to have great self-confidence that you can figure things out on your own. I took her analysis to heart. Specialties are both necessary (because life is short) and destructive (because they form barriers to entry). Early statisticians had a motto about the heaps of data they piled up: “aliis exterendum,” to be threshed out by others. Much academic research perpetually postpones the synthesis to someone else who never shows up. It’s the sheerest folly to try synthesis, but also a betrayal of learning and opportunity not to. (The encyclopedia, as Jorge Luís Borges knew, is an absurdist genre.) Though there is a certain defiance in wanting to know what holds the world together at its core, it would be a moral and intellectual defeat to give up. (The heart of the university, I believe, is not the specialties, but general education.) If we accept that life is short, knowledge is more abundant than ever, and it is an enormous privilege to have time to read, write, and think, then what kind of work should we do? The book is one answer to that question. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j164yz7ynwa0q69gedv11aq0))
